Explanation:&nbsp;The user satisfaction index will be computed from user surveys. A baseline survey will be conducted the first year of the project and follow-up surveys in years three and five. This indicator will measure the change in percentage points from baseline.

&nbsp;

â€‹â€‹â€‹â€‹â€‹â€‹â€‹Achievement: &nbsp;The user satisfaction index will be computed from user satisfaction surveys. &nbsp;The overall actual aggregate or average value is increased from the baseline value of 0 to 2 percent. &nbsp;Ghana increased from 0 to 3.3 percent; Cabo Verde increased from 0 to 12 percent (the first survey 50.50 percent was used as a baseline&nbsp;and the recent survey value was 62.2 satisfaction); Cote d&#39;Ivoire (74.7 percent satisfaction is regstered to be used as a baseline for the next report); Liberia 60 percent was reported as per the first survey, this will be used as a baseline for the next report; In Sierra Leone, Togo, Burkina Faso the first survey will be conducted soon and the value will be ued as a baseline to measure the progress in the upcoming satisfaction survey (before the current project closing date).

&nbsp;

The actual value reported under Togo 11.43 percent was wrongly entered. It should be equal to the baseline value.

Explanation: The list datasets include (1) an Anonymized cartography dataset; (2) an Anonymized household poverty survey, (3) a CPI dataset, (4) an Anonymized agricultural census or annual survey dataset, (5) an Anonymized updated business registry, (6) Input-output tables, and (7) Anonymized labor force surveys dataset. Open access means available to the users on a website or upon request. Statistics are comparable when produced using similar methodologies.
&nbsp;

&nbsp;

A chievement: Share of comparable core economic and social statistics datasets produced and available for open access. The aggregate or average value is increased from 24 &nbsp;to 37 percent. Ghana produced 2 out of 7 datasets (29 percent), which increased from 14 to 29 percent; Liberia produced 3 out of 7 (43 percent), which increased from the baseline value of 0 to 43 percent; Sierra Leon maintained the baseline value; Togo produced 2 out of 7 (29 percent) no change from the previous actual value; Cabo Verde produced 1 out of 7 (14 percent) the previous actual value of 71.4 was wrongly calculated; Burkina Faso produced 5 out of 7 (71 percent) inceased from the baseline value of 0 to 71 percent; and Cote d&#39;Ivoire produced 5 out of 7 (71 percent) increased from 57 to 71 percent.

Explanation:&nbsp;The average number of months between the end of data collection of major surveys and the release of the main results. The surveys to be considered are household surveys, LFS, agriculture surveys, and enterprise surveys.

&nbsp;

Achievement: The aggregate performance was maintained at 11 months. In Ghana the number of months between the end of data collection of major surveys and the release of the main results increased by .3 (8 to 8.3) compared to therevious actual value; in Liberia decreased by 2 (12 to 10), no change in Sierra Leone decreased by 2 (12 to 10), in Togo decreased from 10 to 7; in Cabo Verde decreased from 8.8 to 8; in Burkina Faso decreased from 10 to 6; and in Cote D&#39;Ivoire increased from 14.66 to 23.91.&nbsp;

Explanation:&nbsp; Number of UNSD Minimum List gender indicators available using data collected within the past 5 years.

&nbsp;

â€‹â€‹â€‹â€‹â€‹â€‹â€‹Achievement: The aggregate performance of the UNSD minimum list of gender indicators available using data collected within the past 5 years increased from 144 to 166 (Sierra Leone, Cote d&#3;Ivoire, Cabo Verde, and Burkina exceeded the target value). &nbsp;The number of UNSD minimum list of gender indicators available using data collected in Togo has no changed, Ghana the previous actual value of 62 was entered wrongly, the current value is&nbsp;23; in Sierra Leone increased from 10 to 25; in Liberia no change; in Cote d&#39;Ivoire, no change from the previous value and exceeded the target value; Cabo Verde increased from 20 to 40; and Burkina Faso increased from 17 to 43.

Explanation: A calendar of dissemination of the main statistical products (CPI, national accounts, labor statistics, agricultural production, etc.) is developed and adopted.

&nbsp;

Achievement: In Ghana, Cabo Verde, Burkina Faso, and Cote d&#39;Ivoire the data release calendar of dissemination of the main statistical products is updated regularly. &nbsp;The implementation in Sierra Leone, Liberia, and Togo is on track to meet the target.

